Q1. What is the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)?
Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) states that

"Objects of a subclass should be replaceable
with objects of the superclass without affecting
the correctness of the program."

In simple words,

A child class should behave like its parent
so that it can safely replace the parent
without breaking the application.

Why do we need LSP?

• Ensures correct runtime behavior.
• Makes Runtime Polymorphism reliable.
• Prevents unexpected runtime errors.
• Improves Maintainability.
• Improves Extensibility.

Real-Life Example

A wall socket works with different chargers.

Phone Charger

↓

Laptop Charger

↓

TV Charger

All chargers can be substituted because
they follow the same standard.

